WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.520 --> 00:00:05.460
Cheat codes have been around for almost as
long as there have been video games.

00:00:05.460 --> 00:00:11.020
Whether you want infinite ammo or just want
to give everyone in the game a big stupid

00:00:11.020 --> 00:00:13.860
head, chances are there's a cheat code for
it.

00:00:13.860 --> 00:00:16.620
But how did these magic codes first come about?

00:00:16.620 --> 00:00:19.080
How did word of their existence spread?

00:00:19.080 --> 00:00:24.270
There's a lot of history behind cheat codes,
and it's about time we took a look.

00:00:24.270 --> 00:00:25.270
The POKE

00:00:25.270 --> 00:00:30.010
Cheat codes got their start in the early days
of the home console market when tech-savvy

00:00:30.010 --> 00:00:35.340
gamers would modify a game's code by hand
to alter the gameplay experience.

00:00:35.340 --> 00:00:41.320
It was a long, horrible process that involved
scrubbing through thousands of lines of code

00:00:41.320 --> 00:00:45.930
— or POKES, as they were called — and
changing values at random.

00:00:45.930 --> 00:00:50.820
Then, you'd cross your fingers and hope that
whatever you changed resulted in something

00:00:50.820 --> 00:00:53.150
useful happening in the game.

00:00:53.150 --> 00:00:53.880
"You ready?"

00:00:53.880 --> 00:00:55.100
"Yeah."

00:00:55.160 --> 00:00:56.060
"Let's go!"

00:00:59.180 --> 00:01:03.990
It was pretty much hours of trial and error
just so you could quickly and easily mash

00:01:03.990 --> 00:01:07.270
some buttons to get a few extra lives.

00:01:07.270 --> 00:01:08.270
Developer shortcuts

00:01:08.270 --> 00:01:13.310
While most cheat codes are just ways to give
yourself an unfair advantage, that wasn't

00:01:13.310 --> 00:01:15.060
always their intention.

00:01:15.060 --> 00:01:19.600
Developers often added cheats into their games
to make the games easier to test.

00:01:19.600 --> 00:01:21.920
As developer Chris Sorrell explained:

00:01:21.920 --> 00:01:26.380
"You can't keep fighting the boss just to
check if he correctly drops the special key

00:01:26.380 --> 00:01:31.590
as he dies, so you add a weapon super-power
feature to kill him in one hit.

00:01:31.590 --> 00:01:36.409
Spending just a little time hooking up powerful
cheats means that you save yourself — and

00:01:36.409 --> 00:01:41.189
everyone else working on a project — hundreds
of hours of productivity time."

00:01:41.189 --> 00:01:42.209
That makes sense.

00:01:42.209 --> 00:01:47.380
But sometimes, these cheats were left in the
game by accident, only to be discovered by

00:01:47.380 --> 00:01:49.560
gamers sometime later.

00:01:49.560 --> 00:01:54.649
One of the most famous debug cheats that made
it into the final release was in a game called

00:01:54.649 --> 00:01:59.439
Manic Miner, released on the ZX Spectrum in
1983.

00:01:59.439 --> 00:02:04.539
Designer Matthew Smith used his driver's license
number as the cheat code to unlock a secret

00:02:04.539 --> 00:02:09.190
level-select mode, allowing players to jump
to any level.

00:02:09.190 --> 00:02:10.310
Mainstream cheats

00:02:10.310 --> 00:02:14.240
Soon, cheat codes made their way into video
game magazines.

00:02:14.240 --> 00:02:19.200
Almost every gaming mag on the market had
a section dedicated to listing every known

00:02:19.200 --> 00:02:22.490
cheat code of the most popular games of the
month.

00:02:22.490 --> 00:02:27.250
Eventually, there was even a magazine solely
dedicated to cheat codes, titled Tips and

00:02:27.250 --> 00:02:28.250
Tricks.

00:02:28.250 --> 00:02:33.030
As the codes became more popular, developers
began intentionally sticking cheats into games

00:02:33.030 --> 00:02:37.180
as Easter eggs to give dedicated players an
extra layer of fun.

00:02:37.180 --> 00:02:42.030
Of course, it was only a matter of time before
things went too far...

00:02:42.030 --> 00:02:43.030
The Konami Code

00:02:43.030 --> 00:02:48.380
If you call yourself a gamer, you probably
know this cheat code by heart:

00:02:48.380 --> 00:02:50.010
"OK, OK, try this.

00:02:50.010 --> 00:02:54.910
Hit up, up, down, down, left, right, left,
right, B, A, start and then we'll have unlimited

00:02:54.910 --> 00:02:55.400
lives."

00:02:55.400 --> 00:03:00.460
The Konami Code is the granddaddy of all cheat
codes, but it wasn't originally created with

00:03:00.470 --> 00:03:06.870
the general gaming public in mind: designer
Kazuhisa Hashimoto first put it in Konami's

00:03:06.870 --> 00:03:13.270
1985 classic side-scrolling shooter Gradius,
because without it, there was no way he could

00:03:13.270 --> 00:03:14.960
beat his own game.

00:03:14.960 --> 00:03:19.760
Just a few years later, the same code was
used in the development of another Konami

00:03:19.760 --> 00:03:21.490
hit, Contra.

00:03:21.490 --> 00:03:28.290
Entering it gave players 30 lives rather than
3, which made the game...slightly easier to

00:03:28.290 --> 00:03:29.290
beat.

00:03:29.290 --> 00:03:33.760
The code became so well-known that Konami
went on to add it into dozens of its titles

00:03:33.760 --> 00:03:37.740
— and it's shown up in tons of video games
ever since.

00:03:37.740 --> 00:03:38.740
Law-breakers

00:03:38.740 --> 00:03:44.870
The 1990s were a golden era for cheat codes,
and some developers used that to their advantage.

00:03:44.870 --> 00:03:49.069
When Mortal Kombat came under fire for its
graphic depictions of violence, its developers

00:03:49.069 --> 00:03:55.180
were forced to remove the game's nastier bits
for its Sega Genesis release — sort of.

00:03:55.180 --> 00:04:00.061
Mortal Kombat for the Genesis was free of
blood and guts…until this screen came up.

00:04:00.061 --> 00:04:06.770
If a player entered the cheat code A, B, A,
C, A, B, B, it reverted game from its cleaned-up,

00:04:06.770 --> 00:04:10.660
family-friendly version to its bloody arcade
roots.

00:04:17.400 --> 00:04:19.019
Cheat codes get weird

00:04:19.019 --> 00:04:24.849
As cheats grew more popular, they became more
about players being able to alter games in

00:04:24.849 --> 00:04:26.750
strange, bizarre ways.

00:04:26.750 --> 00:04:28.879
Case in point: big head mode.

00:04:28.879 --> 00:04:34.129
No one is quite sure which game was the first
to include this ridiculous cheat, but credit

00:04:34.129 --> 00:04:38.110
for popularizing it goes to the NBA Jam series.

00:04:38.110 --> 00:04:43.189
Big head mode literally made characters' heads
much larger than normal...and that's it.

00:04:43.189 --> 00:04:47.860
It gave players no greater strategic advantage,
but it would later appear in in hundreds of

00:04:47.860 --> 00:04:53.599
games, even making appearances in games like
Microsoft's Gears of War series and Batman:

00:04:53.599 --> 00:04:54.599
Arkham Knight.

00:04:54.599 --> 00:04:57.259
But that's not where the cheat code weirdness
ended.

00:04:57.259 --> 00:05:02.289
While the most useful codes gave players unfair
advantages, others, like some featured in

00:05:02.289 --> 00:05:05.949
Grand Theft Auto III, actually did the opposite.

00:05:05.949 --> 00:05:10.260
Developers Rockstar added a code that made
all the citizens of Liberty City go into a

00:05:10.260 --> 00:05:15.330
blind rage from the moment they saw the main
character, compelling them to attack the player

00:05:15.330 --> 00:05:17.800
on sight.

00:05:17.800 --> 00:05:18.800
Achievements

00:05:18.800 --> 00:05:23.139
Cheat codes don't quite fit into the modern
gaming world the way they used to.

00:05:23.139 --> 00:05:27.990
According to video game journalist Dan Amrich,
the core satisfaction gamers got when they

00:05:27.990 --> 00:05:33.500
used cheat codes was eventually duplicated
by the achievement systems on Xbox Live, the

00:05:33.500 --> 00:05:35.610
PlayStation Network, and Steam.

00:05:35.610 --> 00:05:36.779
Amrich told Vice:

00:05:36.779 --> 00:05:42.389
"Cheat codes were no longer the currency of
hardcore gamers once Achievements appeared.

00:05:42.389 --> 00:05:47.069
They were another creative outlet for dev
teams and they were required by Microsoft,

00:05:47.069 --> 00:05:51.349
so the effort that would go into cheats went
into Achievements instead."

00:05:51.349 --> 00:05:56.120
Why brag about your knowledge or usage of
the cheat code when you can brag about the

00:05:56.120 --> 00:06:00.849
things you've been able to accomplish in a
game without cheating?

00:06:00.849 --> 00:06:02.499
It's a modder's world

00:06:02.499 --> 00:06:04.620
Cheat codes are far from extinct.

00:06:04.620 --> 00:06:07.279
They'll always exist in some form or another.

00:06:07.279 --> 00:06:12.639
Maybe not as a series of button-mashing combos,
but as something else entirely.

00:06:12.639 --> 00:06:17.580
After all, if you can mod Skyrim to make your
character invincible and turn all the dragons

00:06:17.580 --> 00:06:21.639
into Thomas the Tank Engine, you're definitely
still using cheats.

00:06:21.639 --> 00:06:25.379
And as long as you're having fun doing it,
more power to you.

00:06:25.379 --> 00:06:26.379
Thanks for watching!

00:06:26.379 --> 00:06:29.229
Click the Looper icon to subscribe to our
YouTube channel.

00:06:29.229 --> 00:06:32.069
Plus check out all this cool stuff we know
you'll love, too!

